{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices during downtrends (often by ~2–8 €/t, occasionally >10 €/t). Apply a downward level shift when the last 10–20 days show a clear negative slope.",
    "Trend-lag / underestimation of selloffs: the model reacts too slowly to regime breaks (sharp declines). When recent returns are strongly negative (e.g., multiple consecutive down days or large 5–10d drawdown), steepen the near-term downward path (front-load declines) rather than spreading them out.",
    "Over-optimistic mean reversion: forecasts repeatedly revert upward into the mid/upper 70s even while the realized path continues lower (into high 60s/low 60s). Reduce mean-reversion strength and anchor forecasts closer to the latest level when momentum is negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: errors grow with horizon; the back half of the 30d forecast tends to overshoot the eventual level most. Apply a horizon-increasing downward adjustment (small in days 1–5, larger by days 15–30) in bearish regimes.",
    "Cap rebound amplitude after drops: when the model projects rebounds (local peaks) following declines, realized rebounds are smaller and shorter-lived. Damp forecasted upswings (lower peaks, faster fade) after any recent sharp selloff."
  ]
}
